  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said:",
  "passage": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-convicti…",
  "statement": "The backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate"
 },
 {
  "id": "S011",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0",
  "title": "Nvidia acts as backstop for customer GPUs in return for cut of cloud revenue",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said:",
  "passage": "… said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\"\nThis is not the first time Nvidia has signed deals like this, having previously done so with the likes of CoreWeave and Lambda in 2025.\nThe agreement with CoreWeave was signed in September 2025 and had an initial value of $6.3bn , while the deal with Lambda was valued at $1.5bn .\nContracts like these ensure that Nvidia maintains a diversified customer base and also expands its internal compute capacity for researchers when GPUs are left idle.\nThe newer contracts have the added benefit of generating income for Nvidia based on the neoclouds' success, effectively creating a win-win situation.",
  "statement": "The agreement with CoreWeave was signed in September 2025 and had an initial value of $6.3bn"
 },
 {
  "id": "S012",
  "article_id": "gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd",
  "title": "Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Next AI Rack Costs Nearly Double the Last One, and Memory Is Why. What Infrastructure Buyers Should Budget For.\nNvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin rack will cost hyperscalers $7.8 million—nearly double the prior generation—with memory now accounting for 25-30% of total cost instead of 5-10%. This shift signals that AI infrastructure budgeting must focus on total system cost rather than GPU pricing alone.\nThis story was produced through MarketScale . See how Software & Technology teams ",
  "passage": "…unt for\nThe single most expensive part of Nvidia's next-generation AI rack is no longer just the GPU. According to a Morgan Stanley bill-of-materials analysis, memory now accounts for roughly a quarter of the total, and the overall system price has nearly doubled generation over generation. For any organization budgeting AI infrastructure for the second half of 2026 and beyond, that shift changes how the numbers should be built.\nMorgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation. The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\nThe memory line is where the cost concentrates. Morgan Stanley puts memory content per rack at about $2 million, a 435% jump from the prior generation, driven by a threefold increase in LPDDR5X capacity to 54 terabytes per rack plus roughly $1 million or more in 3D NAND storage that was virtually absent from earlier systems. First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\nAs a share of the bill of materials, memory rose from 5 to 10 percent on the GB200 to 25 to 30 percent on the VR200, while the GPU's share fell from roughly 63 percent to 51 percent.\nOnly a handful of companies buy racks at this scale. But the force behind the price, constrai…",
  "statement": "The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume."
 },
 {
  "id": "S013",
  "article_id": "gnews:d5c48abb470f394f86549b6b",
  "title": "Nvidia underperforms peers as report sparks rack delay worries",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Investing.com -- NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) shares ticked up just 0.8% on Monday, starkly trailing a 3.2% surge in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index and lagging rivals Advanced Micro Devices Inc (NASDAQ:AMD), which gained 7.7%, and Broadcom Inc (NASDAQ:AVGO), which jumped 4.4%. The underperformance came as a broad rally for chipmakers collided with a boutique research report alleging significant delays in the AI giant's next-generation server architectures.\nSupply chain research firm Se",
  "passage": "…ts following massive year-to-date run-ups. Japan's Ibiden Co., a PCB maker that counts Nvidia as its largest client, dropped 8.4% by close, while Hong Kong-listed Kingboard Laminates Holdings Ltd. tumbled 12.6% and South Korea's Samsung Electro-Mechanics Co. slid 8.1%.\nAccording to analysts cited by Bloomberg, these rumored technical setbacks could severely cap the scale-up capacity of Nvidia's future hardware. This perceived vulnerability may create a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms, specifically AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\nMizuho analyst Jordan Klein dismissed such fears in his daily \"Tech Bytes\" note, calling the product delay stories \"more noise.\" \"I would not be worried about NVDA nor looking to sell the stock,\" Klein said.\nThe analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\"\nThe lackluster session keeps the world's most valuable public company squarely rangebound as investors navigate an increasingly jittery AI market. Just last week, erratic headlines regarding Meta Platforms' AI spending ambitions sent tech stocks on a whipsaw ride, underscoring growing market anxiety in the AI trade.\nUltimately, the market's outsized reaction to the…",
  "statement": "\"I would not be worried about NVDA nor looking to sell the stock,\" Klein said."
 },
 {
  "id": "S014",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balanc",
  "passage": "…ials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deep…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032"
 },
 {
  "id": "S015",
  "article_id": "gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb",
  "title": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-14",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\nBy Jowi Morales\nIt's now harder to buy Nvidia AI ",
  "passage": "… months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China. This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe. Nvidia was not immediately available for comment on the news.\nAlthough the U.S. has banned the latest AI GPUs for export into China since 2022, various investigations showed Chinese companies could still easily get their hands on these coveted chips until recently. Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need. President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough. One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\nAs Nvidia reportedly cleaned up its verified list of clients and made it harder for non-vetted companies to acquir…",
  "statement": "Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need."
 },
 {
  "id": "S016",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "The largest company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI bu",
  "passage": "… company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI build-out is as strong as ever\nNvidia makes graphics processing units (GPUs) along with various products to support its accelerated computing ecosystem. GPUs are still the top choice for running AI workloads, and Nvidia dominates the market share of GPUs sold for data center computing. There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\nThis year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\nThat's a major increase and should boost Nvidia's revenue along the way. In July, all of the big four AI hyperscalers report earnings, which will likely include commentary surrounding AI spending and how these companies are monetizing AI. I'll be paying attention to all of them, but the biggest one I'll be watching i…",
  "statement": "Nvidia dominates the market share of GPUs sold for data center computing"
 },
 {
  "id": "S017",
  "article_id": "nvblog:performance-per-watt-ai-infrastructure-efficiency",
  "title": "Why Performance per Watt Is the Ultimate Metric for AI Infrastructure Efficiency | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-14",
  "article_opening": "From benchmark to production, NVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 delivers the highest performance per watt to maximize revenue and the lowest token cost to maximize profit margins.\nPower is AI infrastructure’s inescapable constraint. How many tokens an AI factory can generate within a fixed power budget determines its revenue and profitability. Because of this, performance per watt — a metric that can’t be gamed, only earned through real-world results — is the foundation for AI factories.\nAs agentic AI driv",
  "passage": "…dling them requires engineering rigor and time in production.\nNVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 systems continues to set the standard across a diverse range of models and production use cases delivering sustained performance, rack-level reliability and economics that hold under real traffic day after day.\nThat’s why leading AI labs such as Anthropic, OpenAI and SpaceXAI use NVIDIA Blackwell NVL72 systems to run inference.\nIn addition, a variety of inference service providers and AI natives use the Blackwell platform to deploy open models in production.\nCoreWeave has deployed Kimi K2.6 on NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, combining NVFP4 quantization and EAGLE3 speculative decoding to maximize inference performance.\nPerplexity runs Qwen3 235B and post-trained Qwen3.5-397B-A17B on NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 for its AI agent platform, serving millions of queries daily with the latency and reliability that consumers need.\nFireworks AI deploys GLM 5.2 on the NVIDIA Blackwell platform, enabling production deployments for customers including Cursor and Factory AI.\nThis accumulated production experience, built across generations of frontier models and real-world deployments, is what gives NVIDIA Vera Rubin its head start.\nLearn more about the NVIDIA Vera Rubin platform in this technical blog and find details on the NVIDIA DSX AI factory-scale platform and DSX MaxLPS .",
  "statement": "Perplexity runs Qwen3 235B and post-trained Qwen3.5-397B-A17B on NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 for its AI agent platform, serving millions of queries daily"
 },
 {
  "id": "S018",
  "article_id": "gnews:07610e55de6cc86b2b508b1d",
  "title": "NVIDIA Nemotron Achieves Benchmark-Leading Performance With LangChain Deep Agents Harness",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more t",
  "passage": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more tasks at higher throughput and running at 10x lower inference cost per run than leading closed models.\nMeasured against LangChain’s Deep Agents benchmark, Nemotron 3 Ultra also achieved business task parity with the highest-scoring closed models. No model retraining was required. Every gain came from engineering the environment around the model, not the model itself.\nAt a tenth of the cost, teams harnessing NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra can run evaluations continuously, experiment faster and build specialized agents across more of their business.\nLangChain’s agent engineering platform has more than 200 million monthly downloads. By tuning its Deep Agents harness specifically for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, it allows for high-performing agents that complete more tasks, run faster and give enterprises…",
  "statement": "running at 10x lower inference cost per run than leading closed models"
 },
 {
  "id": "S019",
  "article_id": "gnews:0e3cbf453958ce83fabf7a65",
  "title": "Nvidia and Palantir Are Bringing Sovereign AI to the U.S. Government. Here's How.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "In a move that underscores the growing convergence of advanced computing hardware and enterprise software platforms, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Palantir Technologies ( PLTR +1.34% ) announced a collaboration designed to bring powerful open AI models into highly sensitive, classified environments.\nThe collaboration targets U.S. government agencies -- where data control, security requirements, and customization are nonnegotiable. By combining open-source flexibility with innovative safeguards, Nvi",
  "passage": "…rational frameworks required in sensitive environments.\nWhy does this opportunity matter in the age of AI?\nIn today's evolving AI landscape, raw model performance is not the only bottleneck. For government agencies, some of the biggest challenges involve trust, control, and the ability to operate without exposing sensitive information.\nClosed models carry the risk of unintended data leakage, while fully open models that lack stringent deployment protocols often lack the security and audit features needed for regulated use. By partnering, Nvidia and Palantir directly address this problem by delivering customizable, high-capability models that remain under the government's complete ownership.\nThe U.S. government civilian workforce is around 2 million employees across critical sectors such as energy, transportation, healthcare, defense, and financial services. While the business impact of sovereign AI is hard to predict at this stage, Palantir's and Nvidia's focus on one of the largest customer bases -- the public sector -- in regulated AI suggests a sizable and durable market opportunity.\nBy enabling government agencies to adopt frontier models in isolated computer systems while retaining ownership and the ability to continuously improve them, Palantir and Nvidia are helping to remove operational barriers to broader AI integration. In the long run, open models may bring meaningful cost efficiencies to the public sector given their layered advantages in security and cust…",
  "statement": "The U.S. government civilian workforce is around 2 million employees across critical sectors"
 },
 {
  "id": "S020",
  "article_id": "gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c",
  "title": "TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-10",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\n52-Week High: $236\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua ",
  "passage": "…adership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\nAnalyst Joshua Buchalter said Nvidia’s message to investors is clear: computing power remains in short supply, and the market is thinking about Nvidia’s technology far too narrowly.\nHe pointed out that Nvidia’s hardware and software work together, or “co-design,” in a way that gives the company a real edge as AI keeps evolving from general chatbot-style applications toward more specialized, industry-specific tools.\nTo put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.\nMeanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range.\nThat’s a meaningful chunk of business coming from outside the flashy AI chatbot headlines, which is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated.\nTD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\nData center re…",
  "statement": "TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range"
 },
 {
  "id": "S021",
  "article_id": "gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a",
  "title": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature\nBy Jonathan Sayers\nTraditionally, data center operators have recommended an ambient temperature of 18 to 27 degrees Celsius (approximately 64 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit) for data center equipment. But in an unexpected twist, Nvidia's new 100% liquid-cooled AI data centers are running at a feverish temperature of 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit). This may seem counterintuitive, but Nvidia asserts that this c",
  "passage": "…e, and the process doesn't cause them to degrade. This is a huge boon for data centers, considering that every one-degree-Fahrenheit increase in operating temperature translates to up to 5% savings in energy costs. And with the U.S. pushing for data centers to pay for grid access , the timing couldn't be better for Nvidia.\nWhat NVIDIA's high-temperature cooling means for consumers\nThe mention of an all-liquid-cooling data center may raise alarms for everyday people who are concerned about AI data centers doing more harm than just raising prices . These data centers are already consuming a reported 5 million gallons of water per day, to the potential detriment of nearby communities. However, Nvidia claims that its DSX AI factories have \"zero water consumption\" due to a dry-cooler-based design featuring a closed-loop system with no evaporative water cooling.\nWith that being said, this energy-efficient high-temperature cooling method might find its way into your home computer someday. For the CPU and GPU in your home computer, manufacturers explain that you should target a temperature as low as 30 degrees Celsius (86 degrees Fahrenheit) when the system is idle or under light use. That number might hit the new 45-degree standard if Nvidia's partner companies, such as cooling system manufacturer Motivair, start to implement similar engineering into consumer products.\nIf Nvidia is saving big on energy costs at the data center, does that mean the price…",
  "statement": "Nvidia claims that its DSX AI factories have \"zero water consumption\""
 },
 {
  "id": "S022",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…l family for autonomous vehicle development, NVIDIA Isaac GR00T for robotics and NVIDIA BioNeMo for biomedical help accelerate research and development across industries.\nThe momentum extends beyond NVIDIA’s own research labs .\nBasecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids, while 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to …",
  "statement": "KiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90%"
 },
 {
  "id": "S023",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nemotron-langchain-agents-open-stack",
  "title": "NVIDIA Nemotron Achieves Benchmark-Leading Performance With LangChain Deep Agents Harness | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "Tuning the LangChain Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra delivers leading performance and faster task completion on an open stack that enterprises can run, customize and control.\nNVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra is offering leading performance at lower cost than top closed models with the largest and most widely adopted AI agent orchestration platform.\nLangChain tuned its Deep Agents harness for NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Ultra, achieving the highest accuracy among open models, while completing more t",
  "passage": "…, end to end. They can customize it around the expertise that sets their business apart, keep improving it and run it anywhere — their own infrastructure, their own cloud, their own governance.\nThat distinction matters more as agents take on higher-stakes work. The shift from AI assistants that answer questions to agents that take action inside core systems changes what businesses get from their AI.\nNemoClaw for LangChain Deep Agents and the tuned Nemotron 3 Ultra model profile are available now . Developers can pull the tuned Deep Agents harness directly from LangChain, or use the NemoClaw for LangChain Deep Agents blueprint as a starting point for building specialized agents from scratch.\nLangChain developers can access Nemotron 3 Ultra on Baseten , Crusoe Cloud , DeepInfra, Fireworks , Nebius and Together AI platforms, giving them a direct, hosted path to the tuned harness in production.\nEY can help enterprises start building their own specialized agents today, using this open software stack.\nLearn more about NVIDIA NemoClaw for LangChain Deep Agents and NVIDIA Nemotron.",
  "statement": "LangChain developers can access Nemotron 3 Ultra on Baseten , Crusoe Cloud , DeepInfra, Fireworks , Nebius and Together AI platforms"
 },
 {
  "id": "S024",
  "article_id": "edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000060:nvda-20260628.htm",
  "title": "NVDA 8-K 8-K",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "nvda-20260628\n0001045810 2026-06-28 2026-06-28\nDate of Report (Date of earliest event reported): June 28, 2026\nOn June 28, 2026, Ajay K. Puri, Executive Vice President, Worldwide Field Operations of NVIDIA Corporation, or the Company, notified the Company of his intention to retire from his role effective upon the employment commencement date of his successor. Mr. Puri, a 21-year executive at NVIDIA, is expected to remain with the Company in a senior advisory role to ensure a seamless transition",
  "passage": "…icted stock units, or RSUs, with a target value of $35,000,000, vesting approximately over a four-year period according to the Company’s standard new hire vesting schedule, and (b) multi-year performance-based RSUs, or MY PSUs, with a target value of $5,000,000, which will be eligible to vest based on the Company’s total shareholder return relative to the Standard & Poor’s 500 Composite Index over a three-year period. The terms of the RSUs and MY PSUs will be governed by the Company's Amended and Restated 2007 Equity Incentive Plan and applicable RSU and MY PSU agreements. Mr. Parker will be eligible to participate in the Company's benefit programs available to similarly situated employees.\nMr. Parker will enter into the Company’s indemnity agreement, which would require the Company to indemnify Mr. Parker, under the circumstances and to the extent provided for therein, against certain expenses and other amounts incurred by Mr. Parker as a result of being made a party to certain actions, suits, proceedings and the like by reason of his position as an officer of the Company.\nDate: July 2, 2026",
  "statement": "Mr. Parker will enter into the Company’s indemnity agreement"
 },
 {
  "id": "S025",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…ling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) shares actually rose more than 1% on Monday — helped by the company’s denial and a Goldman Sachs note calling the valuation compelling — leaving its market cap around $4.7 trillion. Overseas, PCB suppliers to NVIDIA came under pressure, with reports of sharp intraday declines in names such as Japan’s Ibiden and Hong Kong’s Kingboard Laminates Holdings.\nKyber represents NVIDIA’s shift from selling chips to selling complete AI infrastructure. A single Kyber NVL144 cabinet houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs operating as one unified computing platform, targeting hyperscalers like Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon. A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis.\nThe reported culprit is a component most retail investors have not heard of: the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA also calls the orthogonal backplane), a printed circuit board with up to 78 layers that connects electronic modules within the rack. SemiAnalysis said it “remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.” Stacking 78 layers with signal integrity, thermal tolerance, and yield at volume is a hard engineering problem, and SemiAnalysis frames it as the gating item for NVIDIA’s most ambitious system.\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits. Data Center revenue reach…",
  "statement": "A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis."
 },
 {
  "id": "S026",
  "article_id": "gnews:beb97ecf3fbd192e0636c2db",
  "title": "Jensen Huang Slammed a $2.5 Billion Chip-Smuggling Scheme at Nvidia's Stockholder Meeting",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-13",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has solidified its position as one of the most important companies in the tech world, as the undisputed leader in artificial intelligence (AI)-related hardware. The company started as a graphics card maker for video games, but its graphics processing units (GPUs) and other advanced AI chips have since become the hardware foundation for the current AI boom.\nUnfortunately, there has been a $2.5 billion chip-smuggling scheme on the black market, and Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang isn",
  "passage": "… has a monopoly on the advanced GPUs needed to train and deploy AI. Companies like Amazon and Alphabet are beginning to make their own in-house chips, but for the most part, Nvidia comfortably dominates the market. It won't last forever, but other companies have lots of ground to make up before catching up to Nvidia.\nArguably its biggest obstacle right now, though, is government restrictions and compliance requirements. The U.S. has already implemented strict export bans on certain chips to China, so Huang's taking this stance is a way to stay in the good graces of the U.S. government and avoid further crackdowns or potential fines. The fewer geopolitical and regulatory worries, the better.\nThe black-market chips haven't had much of a negative effect on Nvidia's business. In its most recent quarter (ended April 26), it made $81.6 billion in revenue (up 85% year over year) and $58.3 billion in net income (up 211% year over year).\nNvidia is a well-oiled machine, and this shows just how wide its technological and competitive moat is. That should be encouraging news for investors seeking sustainable growth and who may have had \"AI bubble\" worries. There's a reason the company was comfortable authorizing an $80 billion share buyback program and increasing its dividend from $0.01 to $0.25.",
  "statement": "The black-market chips haven't had much of a negative effect on Nvidia's business."
 },
 {
  "id": "S027",
  "article_id": "gnews:4f3a1d09e90b8fec9c86d2ba",
  "title": "Nvidia RTX 5090 GPUs are so expensive that Intel's Arc Pro B70 is now a genuine bargain for AI — 128GB 4-card configuration costs less than $3800",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia RTX 5090 GPUs are so expensive that Intel's Arc Pro B70 is now a genuine bargain for AI — 128GB 4-card configuration costs less than $3800\nBy Rahim Amir\nA memory-centric bargain?\nNvidia 's RTX 5090 is a uniquely capable GPU - the company's highest-end consumer-grade GPU to date features a whopping 21,760 CUDA cores and 32GB of GDDR7 memory in its cut-down GB202 configuration.\nIt also happens to be one of the most expensive GPUs retail consumers can buy in 2026, going for as much as two ti",
  "passage": "…ons, however: models that require more compute and bandwidth versus memory will lean toward the RTX 5090, which offers a significant advantage even versus multiple Arc Pro B70 GPUs, but those that require a large amount of memory for their parameters would find a B70 configuration a much more cost-efficient play with access to more memory than a corresponding RTX 5090-based offering.\nNvidia's value proposition, however, runs deeper than the silicon itself, and it is where Intel struggles to find a willing buyer, thanks to better software support on various OSes, CUDA-based software stacks that the Arc Pro cannot emulate, and Nvidia-coded libraries that will not function on Intel's hardware.\nIntel's own offerings (oneAPI, OpenVINO, and IPEX) are improving but are widely considered to be behind even AMD's ROCm stack, which in turn lags behind Nvidia's ecosystem. Despite this, the Arc Pro B70, as noted above, benefits from the lack of a real high-VRAM alternative and remains available close to its stated MSRP, making it a formidable and scalable alternative to Nvidia's heavyweight GPU.",
  "statement": "Intel's own offerings (oneAPI, OpenVINO, and IPEX) are improving but are widely considered to be behind even AMD's ROCm stack, which in turn lags behind Nvidia's ecosystem."
 },
 {
  "id": "S028",
  "article_id": "gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce",
  "title": "NVIDIA Unveils New AI Compute Model, But Michael Burry is Shorting Its Stock",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers.\nThe move arrives as Michael Burry increases bearish positions against NVIDIA, creating a sharp debate over AI growth prospects.\nNVIDIA (NVDA) Price Performance. Source: TradingView\nNVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce ",
  "passage": "…ds and token-intensive applications.\nAI is shifting from model training to always-on token production, and that shift demands a new business model.NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories through revenue-sharing and credit-support. This opens up compute access to the… pic.twitter.com/PSQdTQJTga\n— NVIDIA (@nvidia) July 2, 2026\nThe strategy addresses one of the industry’s biggest challenges: the enormous capital required to build AI infrastructure. By helping partners expand capacity faster, NVIDIA hopes to increase utilization rates while making advanced computing resources more accessible.\nEarly participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\nWhy is Michael Burry Betting Against NVDA\nDespite NVIDIA’s continued momentum, some investors remain skeptical about how long current AI-driven valuations can be sustained. Among the most prominent bears is Michael Burry , the investor known for predicting the 2008 housing market collapse.\nBurry’s latest move goes beyond a general warning about the sector. He disclosed a direct short position on NVIDIA at approximately $198.09 per share, while also establishing bearish positions against Tesla , Applied Materials, Caterpillar, and the iShares Semicon…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs"
 },
 {
  "id": "S029",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…ed 63% with backlog nearly doubling to $460 billion, strengthening its in-house TPU case as NVIDIA's Kyber slip extends to 2028.\nThe AI bottleneck has shifted from chip design to system assembly, yet NVIDIA still commands 81% market share and trades 45% below GuruFocus fair value.\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028. CNBC cited the SemiAnalysis post. NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) shares actually rose more than 1% on Monday — helped by the company’s denial and a Goldman Sachs note calling the valuation compelling — leaving its market cap around $4.7 trillion. Overseas, PCB suppliers to NVIDIA came under pressure, with reports of sharp intraday declines in names such as Japan’s Ibiden and Hong Kong’s Kingboard Laminates Holdings.\nKyber represents NVIDIA’s shift from selling chips to selling complete AI infrastructure. A single Kyber NVL144 cabinet houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs operating as one unified computing platform, targeting hyperscalers like Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon. A larger sibling, the NVL576, designe…",
  "statement": "roadmap is intact"
 },
 {
  "id": "S030",
  "article_id": "gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a",
  "title": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature\nBy Jonathan Sayers\nTraditionally, data center operators have recommended an ambient temperature of 18 to 27 degrees Celsius (approximately 64 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit) for data center equipment. But in an unexpected twist, Nvidia's new 100% liquid-cooled AI data centers are running at a feverish temperature of 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit). This may seem counterintuitive, but Nvidia asserts that this c",
  "passage": "…announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. The idea that a data center must feel like a walk-in freezer is explained away as a misconception. All that was left was to find a way to transition from the hybrid liquid-and-air cooling to a pure liquid-cooling solution.\nSo, how does the Nvidia DSX design work? Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees. It was observed that processors can continue to operate at full performance at this temperature, and the process doesn't cause them to degrade. This is a huge boon for data centers, considering that every one-degree-Fahrenheit increase in operating temperature translates to up to 5% savings in energy costs. And with the U.S. pushing for data centers to pay for grid access , the timing couldn't be better for Nvidia.\nWhat NVIDIA's high-temperature cooling means for consumers\nThe mention of an all-liquid-cooling data center may raise alarms for everyday people who are concerned about AI data centers doing more harm than just raising prices . These data centers are already consuming a reported 5 million gallons of water per day, to the potential detriment of nearby communities. However, Nvidia claims that its DSX AI factories have \"zero water consumption\" due to a dry-cooler-based design featuring a closed-loop system with no evaporative water cooling.\nWith that being said, this energy-efficient …",
  "statement": "every one-degree-Fahrenheit increase in operating temperature translates to up to 5% savings in energy costs"
 },
 {
  "id": "S031",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02",
  "title": "Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company wa",
  "passage": "… sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.”\nNoah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York. He previously wrote about economics and technology at the Council on Foreign Relations. His work has appeared in the Boston Globe and PBS News . He graduated from Georgetown University.\nBy Michael Kovrig\nA new book provides searing testimonials of how the Chinese Communist Party’s thought remolding and coercive interrogations continue under the legal obfuscation of “residential surveillance at a designated location”.\nBy Savannah Billman\nBy Eliot Chen\nThe former State Department official in the Biden administration talks about leading the first talks with China on AI, and what the realistic goals should be of any dialogue between the two superpowers about the...",
  "statement": "Noah Berman is a staff writer for The Wire based in New York"
 },
 {
  "id": "S032",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
  "title": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "…is a different animal. Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron have avoided the aggressive oversupply that crushed memory pricing in prior cycles, a meaningful break from history. Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability. NVIDIA has been smart about locking in its own memory supply as other companies like Apple (Nasdaq: AAPL) have been caught flat footed. And yet, the combined value of Micron, SK Hynix, and Samsung now rivals NVIDIA itself.\nMost strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved. Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027. Simply put, the rise of memory is shifting the center of gravity in the data center trade away from NVIDIA and towards memory names.\nWhich brings the market to the quiet question underneath all of it. Demand looks strong in 2027, but with hyperscalers now having to move into debt and equity offerings, the question of whether growth slows dramatically in 2028 is now front and center on investors minds. The ‘good’ news for NVIDIA investors is the company now trades for just 12X its forecasted 2028 earnings. If a slowdown does come, it’ll likely hit NVIDIA a lot less than names trading for 50X their forecasted 2028 profits.\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alon…",
  "statement": "Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027."
 },
 {
  "id": "S033",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "… open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids, while 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to accelerate the development and validation of their robots.\nExplore NVIDIA’s open models on Hugging Face .\nExplore genomics and biology research at ICML’s GenBio Workshop on Friday, July 10.",
  "statement": "1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab"
 },
 {
  "id": "S034",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout",
  "title": "NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑s",
  "passage": "…s across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.",
  "statement": "AI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed"
 },
 {
  "id": "S035",
  "article_id": "gnews:784c556d2e58ed09dd8aa7fc",
  "title": "Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Hesai Technology, a Shanghai-based manufacturer of the sensors that allow robots and self-driving vehicles to see, was blacklisted as a national security threat in 2024 by the U.S. Department of Defense, which designated Hesai as a Chinese military entity.\nIts presence on the blacklist does not prevent U.S. companies from using Hesai's technology, and the company has been expanding its reach in the U.S., including via a partnership with Nvidia.\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO, says his comp",
  "passage": "…s first extended interview about his company's blacklist designation by the U.S. Department of Defense.\nDespite the federal blacklist designation, Hesai's reach is growing. Under an expanded partnership between Hesai and Nvidia , Hesai sensors will be one of the options automakers can choose to integrate into Nvidia's autonomous vehicle platforms, which the chipmaker hopes will power the self-driving vehicle revolution.\n\"Our vision is that some day, every single car, every single truck will be autonomous. And we have been working towards that future,\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in his keynote address at the Consumer Electronics Show in January, when the expanded partnership was announced.\nHuang has said that robotics, which includes self-driving cars, is the company's second most important growth category after artificial intelligence. In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported automotive revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by the adoption of its self-driving platforms.\nHesai is one of the dominant suppliers in the global autonomous technology ecosystem. Its sensors are integrated into several autonomous systems in addition to Nvidia's, including those of Amazon's robotaxi company, Zoox; autonomous trucking companies such as Waabi and Kodiak; autonomous vehicle technology company Nuro; and agricultural automation firm Agtonomy. The sensors can also be found at New York's John F. Kennedy International Airport, where they monitor passenger and traffic flow at security checkpoints and gate ent…",
  "statement": "Huang has said that robotics, which includes self-driving cars, is the company's second most important growth category after artificial intelligence."
 },
 {
  "id": "S036",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "The largest company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI bu",
  "passage": "…etch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\nThe next month will prove the AI build-out is as strong as ever\nNvidia makes graphics processing units (GPUs) along with various products to support its accelerated computing ecosystem. GPUs are still the top choice for running AI workloads, and Nvidia dominates the market share of GPUs sold for data center computing. There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\nThis year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\nThat's a major increase and should boost Nvidia's revenue along the way. In July, all of the big four AI hyperscalers report earnings, which will likely include commentary surrounding AI spending and how these companies are monetizing AI. I'll be paying attention to all of them, but the biggest one I'll be watching is Microsoft (NASDAQ: MSFT), as its fiscal year ended on June 30. Microsoft will provide investors with fiscal year (FY) 2027 capital expenditures guidance, and a huge jump over last year's figure cou…",
  "statement": "There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs."
 },
 {
  "id": "S037",
  "article_id": "gnews:a96a4cbf6aae35d01d0d285b",
  "title": "Jim Cramer Explains Why You Need to Own NVIDIA Now",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) was among the stocks on Jim Cramer's Mad Money radar as he taught investors how to profit from the upcoming wave of takeovers . Discussing stocks that have declined in the second quarter, Cramer mentioned the company and its possible competitors.\nSix: NVIDIA. NVIDIA, down just over 5% this month. At these levels, the stock sells at an incredibly cheap 22 times earnings. NVIDIA's got an insane amount of business. So what's keeping a lid on the stock? Well, the c",
  "passage": "NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) was among the stocks on Jim Cramer's Mad Money radar as he taught investors how to profit from the upcoming wave of takeovers . Discussing stocks that have declined in the second quarter, Cramer mentioned the company and its possible competitors.\nSix: NVIDIA. NVIDIA, down just over 5% this month. At these levels, the stock sells at an incredibly cheap 22 times earnings. NVIDIA's got an insane amount of business. So what's keeping a lid on the stock? Well, the company has some powerful detractors, mainly clients that feel they charge too much, like Google and Amazon and Tesla. Hey, by the way, they're customers. The first two claim that they have better chips than NVIDIA, even as they use NVIDIA. Plus, there's Broadcom, vicious competitor, and CEO Hock Tan's out there saying it can compete; it can take share from NVIDIA.\nSo how can it change its stock's trajectory? Historically, I'd say they just need to keep reporting breathtaking results. The problem is the spectacular results haven't driven the stock higher. Investors say it can't continue. NVIDIA needs to get many more clients away from the Mag Seven, like sovereign wealth funds… And it needs to buy back stock hand over fist mimicking Apple during its historic run. The stock…",
  "statement": "mainly clients that feel they charge too much"
 },
 {
  "id": "S038",
  "article_id": "gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512",
  "title": "CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek, is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nBig Tech leadership is shifting. The Magnificent 7 and Broadcom weakened in June, while the broader market held up better than expected and Micron surged.\nInvestors should respect both the opportunity and the risk. Micron’s growth, margins, and role in AI memory are impressive, but its ra",
  "passage": "…h is not a lot.\nInterestingly, the S&P 500 is up about 9.5 percent year to date, but over the same period, the forward price-to-earnings ratio has contracted nearly 10 percent. That is because earnings of the companies in that index have been incredible, with roughly an 18 percent growth rate for the first half of 2026, and Q2 expected to be above 20 percent.\nThat trend—rising stock prices and simultaneously improving valuations—is very bullish. I have doubts that the earnings growth rate can continue at that pace, but it is not impossible. I mean, it just happened, so we know it is not impossible.\nFor earnings to keep improving, we need 1) lower oil prices (which seems to be happening), 2) the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months), and 3) interest rates to stay where they are, or go lower (but the futures market says they will go higher). So, two out of three ain’t bad, and it is probably enough to keep the economy from slipping into a recession and keep the stock market from crashing. But two out of three probably isn’t enough for stocks to rocket higher either.\nIt seems worth staying invested, even if the S&P pullback hits double digits in the short term. Unfortunately, that short-term pain is the entry fee for the potential of long-term rewards.\nDespite the weakness in large technology stocks, Micron has had enough positive performance to push it into the coveted trillion-dollar club. Apple was the first company t…",
  "statement": "the AI-related infrastructure buildout to continue (I suspect it will through the next 12 months)"
 },
 {
  "id": "S039",
  "article_id": "gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a",
  "title": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above ",
  "passage": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]\nU.S. export controls bar Chinese designers from accessing the most advanced overseas foundries and high-bandwidth memory, posing a major manufacturing hurdle [2]\nDeepSeek, the Hangzhou-based AI startup behind some of China's most advanced large language models, is developing its own custom AI chip, Reuters reported on Monday, citing three people with knowledge of the matter [1] . The chip is designed for inference — the phase where trained models generate responses to user queries — rather than for training new models.\nThe project has been underway for roughly a year and remains in early stages, with DeepSeek quietly expanding its chip engineering team without public job postings [2] . The …",
  "statement": "DeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above $50 billion, its first outside capital raise [4]"
 },
 {
  "id": "S040",
  "article_id": "gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4",
  "title": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of th",
  "passage": "…s, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.\nThe initiative reflects a shift in the AI market towards production inference.\nNvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers.\nThis model is particularly relevant as the market transitions from model development to production inference, where computing systems operate continuously to deliver AI outputs at scale.\nUnder this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\nNvidia will receive its standard product revenue from infrastructure sales, in addition to a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity. This approach offers AI cloud operators a viable means to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.\nThe initiative refl…",
  "statement": "This model is particularly relevant as the market transitions from model development to production inference"
 },
 {
  "id": "S041",
  "article_id": "gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb",
  "title": "A Utah microreactor just powered an Nvidia AI chip live on stage, a first for next-generation nuclear in America, and the two companies used the moment to announce a 30-megawatt AI factory in the desert designed to drink zero local water",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Valar Atomics just made history in the Utah desert by connecting a next-generation nuclear reactor directly to an Nvidia AI chip — a first in America — and the implications for the data center industry's biggest PR crisis go far beyond one desktop PC drawing power on a stage.\nThe reactor cooling itself with pressurized helium instead of water is the quiet technical detail that changes everything about where AI infrastructure can be built next.\nWith over $130 billion in data center projects alrea",
  "passage": "…r nuclear option isn't just an engineering story — it's a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout.\nBut there's a significant legal and regulatory gap between a DOE demonstration authorization and an actual commercial license, and how Valar navigates that gap will determine whether this moment becomes a milestone or just a very expensive photo op.\nData centers have spent the past year taking the blame for two household problems at once: the power bill and the water supply. In a lot of American towns, the complaints stuck.\nMore than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\nSo the event a nuclear startup and Nvidia staged in the Utah desert on July 1 was aimed squarely at that mood. Valar Atomics wired a Blackwell -based Nvidia desktop unit to its Ward 250 microreactor, served a website off the electricity, and announced a plan for something much bigger that skips the local water entirely.\nOne desktop PC, one reactor, one website\nThe demo itself was almost domestic. On stage at the San Rafael Energy Lab outside Orangeville, a Valar team member plugged an Nvidia Spark desktop unit, running the company’s Blackwell architecture, into a circuit fed from the reactor hall.\nInside that hall sits Ward 250, a TRISO-fueled high-temperature gas reactor cooled with pressu…",
  "statement": "Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home."
 },
 {
  "id": "S042",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02",
  "title": "Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company wa",
  "passage": "…he end of this year, Heim adds.\nHuawei made just 6 percent of the total AI chips sold worldwide during the first quarter of this year, and half that amount after adjusting for the fact that the company’s chips are not as powerful as Nvidia’s, according to Epoch AI, a research institute.\nWhile Nvidia’s report framed its argument on AI chip export controls around its competition with Huawei, others argue that a more important issue is that the restrictions will incentivize Chinese companies to try to catch up with their American rivals.\n“There are good arguments against export controls,” Heim says. But instead of articulating those, Nvidia is “trying to use technical terms to confuse people.”\nNvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\nThe Chinese govern…",
  "statement": "Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe"
 },
 {
  "id": "S043",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "… already has chips it claims are 15 times faster than some of Nvidia's models.\nAn even bigger threat would be if a competitor could challenge the operating system Nvidia has built for developers using its graphics processing units (GPUs) to train AI models, called Compute Unified Device Architecture (CUDA) .\nNvidia launched CUDA in 2006 and has built a whole ecosystem around it, so this is easier said than done, but it nonetheless represents a substantial part of Nvidia's moat and therefore would be devastating to the company if CUDA loses its grip on the market.\nAs you can see in the chart above, Nvidia has had an average forward price-to-earnings ratio of nearly 34 for the past two years. Wall Street consensus estimates suggest the company will earn adjusted earnings per share of $8.97 in its current fiscal year.\nApplying a 34 forward P/E, the stock would be worth roughly $305 per share, implying about 54% upside from current levels. This means a $25,000 investment in Nvidia would be worth over $38,400 if history repeats itself.\nNow, investors should understand that there's no guarantee this plays out. Nvidia, at its current market cap, is up against the law of large numbers. As companies get more mature, they typically face lower multiples. Competition could also be a real concern.\nHowever, Nvidia has traded at a 34 forward P/E before, so it could do so again should the company prove investors wrong about their concerns.\nHere's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats …",
  "statement": "Wall Street consensus estimates suggest the company will earn adjusted earnings per share of $8.97 in its current fiscal year"
 },
 {
  "id": "S044",
  "article_id": "gnews:8d5c91ae833af14257a59b2b",
  "title": "Chinese firms leave Nvidia for local AI suppliers, survey shows",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence surve",
  "passage": "[SINGAPORE] Chinese companies are ditching Nvidia’s advanced accelerators in favour of domestic silicon, underscoring how tensions with the US are reshaping the artificial intelligence infrastructure buildout and propelling Beijing’s ambitions to substitute American technology.\nExecutives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months.\nThis is up from 30 per cent today, according to a Bloomberg Intelligence survey released on Tuesday (Jul 7).\nIn addition, 80 per cent of executives said their total infrastructure spending is running over-budget this year, mostly because of the high cost of AI-related projects.\nChina’s biggest AI infrastructure builders and their key suppliers – Tencent, Alibaba and Huawei Technologies – are best-positioned to take advantage of the shift.\nAI accelerators produced by Hygon Information Technology and Cambricon Technologies were also being evaluated by a large pool of respondents to the survey.\n“China’s drive to substitute locally-made AI semiconductors for foreign ones is making progress, whi…",
  "statement": "Executives in the country say they will allocate 46 per cent of their budget for AI accelerators to domestic products over the next 12 months."
 },
 {
  "id": "S045",
  "article_id": "gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677",
  "title": "Nvidia Corp. (NVDA) Announces a Profit-Sharing Program For Its Computing Framework Access",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is one of the top 10 AI stocks that will skyrocket .\nOn July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-power",
  "passage": "…r this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to their end users.\nIn a blog post, the company's Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer, Colette Kress, defined income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream, while noting that it eliminates the need for location selection, building, energy procurement, and hardware installation before accessing hyperscale computing.\nNvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale. The company also cited that access to high-cost computing resources was restricted historically, even with long-duration commitments.\nThe move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors. Nvidia stock dropped 0.5% in premarket trading on July 2. According to the company statement, the model also intensifies the engagement as compared to ownership stakes in next-generation cloud firms like Nebius and CoreWeave.\nNVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is a computing infrastructure company that has transitioned from PC graphics chips towards full-scale compute and networking solutions. It has now become a leading player within the AI and high-performance computing space. The company offers Data Center acceler…",
  "statement": "The company also cited that access to high-cost computing resources was restricted historically, even with long-duration commitments."
 },
 {
  "id": "S046",
  "article_id": "gnews:6b89157155648b9ec317d209",
  "title": "SambaNova hits $11 billion valuation as investors back Nvidia chip challengers",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "An AI chip startup has raised $1 billion in financing as investors continue to pour money into companies looking to challenge Nvidia .\nSambaNova is now valued at $11 billion thanks to the financing, which was led by General Atlantic along with participation from Seligman Ventures, T. Rowe Price and Capital Group.\nThe latest round of funding, announced Wednesday, comes after the startup raised more than $350 million earlier this year from investors including Intel , with which it also announced a",
  "passage": "…y move fast and drive the business across a broad range of sectors,\" SambaNova's Co-founder and CEO Rodrigo Liang told CNBC at the Raise AI summit in Paris.\n\"We're scaling the business really, really fast, and so the capital allows us to really accelerate the deployments of the racks that customers really want,\" he said.\nLiang added that the company is strongly considering an IPO in 2027, most likely in the U.S.\nSambaNova is one of a slew of startups looking to make waves in the market for inference chips. These are semiconductors designed to run large AI models in a quick and cost-efficient manner, which has become a particular focus for the industry as more complex AI agents are deployed.\nThe company sells its latest chip — the SN50 — as part of a server unit that can be deployed in data centers. This is different to the graphics processing unit (GPU) architecture that Nvidia has sold and has been critical for training huge AI models.\nBut SambaNova is also focused on so-called on-premise deployments where its server units can be installed at a data center owned by a specific company. On Wednesday, JPMorgan Chase said it would deploy SambaNova's systems for \"on-prem inference in our demanding enterprise AI workloads.\"\n\"JPMorgan has selected SambaNova to be the inference provider for the bank,\" Liang said.\n\"For banks and for other industries where data is incredibly important, bringing this infrastructure on prem, bringing this infrastructure with models that are then under your contr…",
  "statement": "The company sells its latest chip — the SN50 — as part of a server unit that can be deployed in data centers."
 },
 {
  "id": "S047",
  "article_id": "gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a",
  "title": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature\nBy Jonathan Sayers\nTraditionally, data center operators have recommended an ambient temperature of 18 to 27 degrees Celsius (approximately 64 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit) for data center equipment. But in an unexpected twist, Nvidia's new 100% liquid-cooled AI data centers are running at a feverish temperature of 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit). This may seem counterintuitive, but Nvidia asserts that this c",
  "passage": "NVIDIA's New Liquid-Cooled Data Centers Are Running At An Unexpected Temperature\nBy Jonathan Sayers\nTraditionally, data center operators have recommended an ambient temperature of 18 to 27 degrees Celsius (approximately 64 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit) for data center equipment. But in an unexpected twist, Nvidia's new 100% liquid-cooled AI data centers are running at a feverish temperature of 45 degrees Celsius (113 degrees Fahrenheit). This may seem counterintuitive, but Nvidia asserts that this cooling method is one of the biggest efficiency leaps in data center history.\nIn Nvidia's blog post by Josh Parker, head of corporate sustainability at Nvidia, it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles. The idea that a data center must feel like a walk-in freezer is explained away as a misconception. All that was left was to find a way to transition from the hybrid liquid-and-air cooling to a pure liquid-cooling solution.\nSo, how does the Nvidia DSX design work? Liquid coolant enters the chip at 45 degrees Celsius, absorbs heat from across the chip's surface, and exits at 55 degrees. It was observed that processors can continue to operate at full performance at this temperature, and the process doesn't cause them to degrade. This is a huge boon for data centers, considering that every one-degree-Fahrenheit increase in operating temperature translates to up to 5% savings in energy costs. A…",
  "statement": "Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles"
 },
 {
  "id": "S048",
  "article_id": "gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677",
  "title": "Nvidia Corp. (NVDA) Announces a Profit-Sharing Program For Its Computing Framework Access",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is one of the top 10 AI stocks that will skyrocket .\nOn July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-power",
  "passage": "…s AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to their end users.\nIn a blog post, the company's Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer, Colette Kress, defined income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream, while noting that it eliminates the need for location selection, building, energy procurement, and hardware installation before accessing hyperscale computing.\nNvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale. The company also cited that access to high-cost computing resources was restricted historically, even with long-duration commitments.\nThe move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors. Nvidia stock dropped 0.5% in premarket trading on July 2. According to the company statement, the model also intensifies the engagement as compared to ownership stakes in next-generation cloud firms like Nebius and CoreWeave.\nNVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is a computing infrastructure company that has transitioned from PC graphics chips towards full-scale compute and networking solutions. It has now become a leading player within the AI and high-performance computing space. The company offers Data Center accelerated computing and networking platforms, along with automotive platforms and electric vehicle solutions.",
  "statement": "The move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors."
