 {
  "id": "S049",
  "article_id": "gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128",
  "title": "Nvidia Launches GPU Backstop Financing Model, Takes Cut of Cloud Revenue From Neocloud Partners",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\nSharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement; Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its ",
  "passage": "…company now directly sharing in its customers' cloud economics.\nThe shift creates a recurring revenue layer on top of Nvidia's hardware sales, potentially smoothing earnings volatility tied to GPU refresh cycles. Analysts have drawn comparisons to GE Capital's equipment financing model, noting both the upside of recurring income and the counterparty risk if neocloud operators face financial stress [4] . Nvidia's blog post positioned the arrangement as providing 'a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream' that diversifies its revenue beyond one-time chip sales [1] .\nNvidia shares traded at $194.83 on July 3, down 1.4% on the session, on a market capitalization of approximately $4.7 trillion. The stock is up 4.5% year-to-date and 22.3% over the past twelve months [5] . The muted reaction suggests investors are still parsing the margin implications of revenue-sharing arrangements versus the benefits of demand-side de-risking.\nSharon AI shares fell sharply, dropping 14.2% to $67.91 on volume of 2.6 million shares. The stock, which IPO'd at roughly $16.55 in February, had rallied to a high of $97.48 in June before the pullback [5] . Firmus Technologies is privately held and does not have a public ticker.\nThe financing model keeps a broader ecosystem of independent GPU cloud providers alive rather than concentrating AI compute inside the hyperscalers — a strategic imperative for Nvidia, which benefits from having multiple distribution channels for its h…",
  "statement": "The stock is up 4.5% year-to-date and 22.3% over the past twelve months"
 },
 {
  "id": "S050",
  "article_id": "gnews:26325b9e051efe16db34f758",
  "title": "Verkada Accelerates Physical AI with NVIDIA",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Verkada is scaling its physical AI platform across more than 2.4 million devices globally with a new technical collaboration and investment from NVIDIA\nSAN MATEO, Calif. , July 1, 2026 /PRNewswire/ -- Verkada , a leader in AI-powered physical security and operations, today announced a collaboration with NVIDIA to accelerate the development and deployment of physical AI across the built environment. NVIDIA also joins as a new investor in Verkada, following a strategic investment from Alphabet's C",
  "passage": "…orkers on factory floors, helps retailers prevent theft, and enables organizations to operate more efficiently.\"\nVerkada is strengthening the models and data flywheel underpinning its intelligent video analytics through its collaboration with NVIDIA, advancing AI-powered video search, multimodal embeddings and vector retrieval for next-generation semantic search, and synthetic data generation to augment training datasets and improve accuracy.\nBy leveraging NVIDIA Cosmos world foundation models and NVIDIA Physical AI Data Factory , Verkada has accelerated model training and inference across its rapidly expanding global footprint on NVIDIA accelerated computing. Since the collaboration began, Verkada has improved the mean average precision (mAP) of its AI-powered search by 68% for spatial-temporal understanding, delivering faster, more accurate, and more robust search capabilities.\nVerkada is also developing a multi-model search agent architecture and exploring reasoning models to address complex, unstructured real-world scenarios — from identifying health and safety incidents on a manufacturing floor to detecting shrinkage in retail environments. The collaborative effort reflects Verkada's broader focus on bringing more capable, context-aware AI to make built environments resilient and safe.\nDesigned with simplicity in mind, Verkada's six product lines — video security cameras , access control , environmental sensors , alarms , workplace , and intercoms — provide unparalleled building security th…",
  "statement": "Verkada has improved the mean average precision (mAP) of its AI-powered search by 68% for spatial-temporal understanding"
 },
 {
  "id": "S051",
  "article_id": "gnews:349140be6b12bf5941071947",
  "title": "Nvidia's RTX 20-series accidentally built GPUs that refuse to die - How-To Geek",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "By Monica J. White\nAsk anyone, and they'll tell you that things like fridges are no longer built to last; they're meant to be replaced fairly regularly. That wasn't the case with GPUs, but I'd argue that we're getting there. However, the RTX 20-series is aging better than many of the cards that came before it.\nThe RTX 20-series crossed the modern GPU feature line early, which is why these GPUs are still useful to this day, while the GTX 10 lineup is growing obsolete .\nThe RTX 20-series made a fe",
  "passage": "…s still mostly a demo.\nThat early feature jump also gave the RTX 20-series a longer runway than its raw performance alone would've suggested. These cards aren't keeping up because they're as fast as the newer GPUs; they're absolutely not. But they can sort of hold their own ground because they can still use DLSS, run ray-traced effects in supported games, and remain part of the driver and software ecosystem that Nvidia is still actively building around.\nAsus GeForce RTX 5070 Ti Prime\n2,482MHz\n16GB\nThe RTX 5070 Ti can easily outpace any RTX 20-series card, but the price is staggering.\nNvidia has killed off pre-RTX 20 cards\nThe gap between the RTX 20-series and its predecessors widens because Nvidia effectively retired the GTX 10-series when it moved Maxwell, Pascal, and Volta GPUs to critical-security-only updates last year.\nMeanwhile, Turing and newer cards are still eligible for Game Ready drivers with bug fixes, new features, and game optimizations. And while legendary cards like the GTX 1080 Ti are still pretty relevant (my own GTX 1060 still works more than fine), they don't have much of a future ahead of them. You can repurpose them for other things , but gaming-wise, they're only going to feel worse by the year.\nDLSS is the real reason RTX 20 still matters\nDLSS is the real reason why these cards still have some breathing room. Simultaneously, it's also the reason why they're starting to be worth replacing.\nNative resolution is brutal on older hardware, especially in newer games, but DLSS lets RTX 20-ser…",
  "statement": "Nvidia effectively retired the GTX 10-series when it moved Maxwell, Pascal, and Volta GPUs to critical-security-only updates last year"
 },
 {
  "id": "S052",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…s like Fab 25. As a result, TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year.\nTo meet this surge, Taipower, Taiwan’s utility, plans to build 18 gas plants totaling about 18.2 GW, roughly 40 percent of Taiwan’s current generating capacity, largely to meet the demand of the semiconductor industry, led by TSMC. This will further lock Taiwan into fossil-fuel dependency, even as the island already relies on fossil fuels for more than 80 percent of its electricity.\nNear Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challenge. Samsung and SK Hynix, the two local giants that produce most of Nvidia’s memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, with little attention to the resulting strain on electricity and emissions. Both companies are key partners in the government’s “Yongin Mega-cluster” plan , a vast complex of semiconductor fabs and AI factories that will become the world’s largest industrial complex.\nShockingly, the plan calls for power from six massive LNG units (3 GW) by 2030 rather than renewables. Yet Yongin will ultimately demand about 15 GW of electricity – roughly one-sixth of South Korea’s total electricity demand – and will consume about half the water used by Seoul and its 10 million people.\nLike Taiwan, South Korea’s energy system is still mostly powered by planet-warming gas. Both countries are critically dependent on the Persian Gulf for their oil and gas, and are now facing their wor…",
  "statement": "are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, with little attention to the resulting strain on electricity and emissions"
 },
 {
  "id": "S053",
  "article_id": "gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9",
  "title": "A $10,000 Investment in Nvidia at the Start of 2026 Is Up Only 5% -- Here's One Promising Sector That Could Reignite Excitement",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "For the first six months of 2026, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.56% ) hasn't led the artificial intelligence (AI) trade. It opened the year at $189.84 on Jan. 2 and closed at around $200 on June 30. That's a roughly 5% gain.\nAnyone who invested $10,000 to start the year hasn't seen much progress in their Nvidia investment. Still, there's a market sector that can open up new revenue opportunities and reward patient Nvidia shareholders.\nWhat a $10,000 investment is worth after the first half of 2026\nIn the las",
  "passage": "…he chipmaker can still reward long-term shareholders, but expectations should be reasonable. Nvidia is not currently offering the kind of monster gains it has been known for in the past.\nAnyone who purchased $10,000 worth of Nvidia stock through fractional investing at its Jan. 2 opening price of $189.84 got a little more than 52 shares. With the closing price of $200 on June 30, that initial $10,000 investment would be worth roughly $10,534.\nWhat could help reignite enthusiasm around Nvidia?\nOne revenue growth opportunity for Nvidia will be in space, and it has already quietly been a behind-the-scenes player through its involvement with Space Exploration Technologies .\nWhen the AI start-up Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units (GPUs). Alphabet has a similar deal, renting compute capacity that gives it access to 110,000 Nvidia GPUs.\nThat involvement with SpaceX will expand further, as Nvidia is set to serve as an initial supplier of hardware for SpaceX's orbital data centers. SpaceX's first version of the satellites it plans to use as data centers in space is called AI1 and will use Nvidia chips.\nAlso, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future. Instead of beaming data back to Earth for processing, this module will run AI computing directly in space.\n\"AI processing across space and ground systems enables real-time sensing, decision-making, and auto…",
  "statement": "Anthropic announced it was renting compute capacity from SpaceX's data center, it revealed that the data center is powered by over 220,000 Nvidia graphics processing units"
 },
 {
  "id": "S054",
  "article_id": "gnews:c0432377817a1c00a1bbadf0",
  "title": "Nvidia AI server delay report sends Asian tech stocks sliding",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia AI server delay report sends Asian tech stocks sliding\nThe stock declines showed investors are wary of any potential negative developments after big runups in AI-related stocks\n[SINGAPORE] Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties.\nResearch firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards fo",
  "passage": "Nvidia AI server delay report sends Asian tech stocks sliding\nThe stock declines showed investors are wary of any potential negative developments after big runups in AI-related stocks\n[SINGAPORE] Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties.\nResearch firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment outside of regular office hours.\nThe report landed as AI stock investors are growing increasingly jittery , with even the smallest setback sparking outsized reactions after the yearslong rally. Last week, global tech stocks whipsawed on headlines hinting at potential overcapacity in the AI buildup and growing competition.\nJapan’s Ibiden, a PCB maker that counts Nvidia as its largest client, dropped as much as 10 per cent. Among related suppliers, Kingboard Laminates Holdings tumbled 18 per cent in Hong Kong, Elite Material fell 10 per cent in Taiwan and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid 11 per cent in South Korea.\nThe SemiAnalysis report spurred …",
  "statement": "Research firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform."
 },
 {
  "id": "S055",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…enerational leap in the ability to build robots, autonomous vehicles and vision AI that perceive, reason, plan and act in the physical world.\nIn addition, the NVIDIA Alpamayo open model family for autonomous vehicle development, NVIDIA Isaac GR00T for robotics and NVIDIA BioNeMo for biomedical help accelerate research and development across industries.\nThe momentum extends beyond NVIDIA’s own research labs .\nBasecamp Research developed a new DNA foundation model, EDEN , that helps researchers interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humano…",
  "statement": "Sakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra"
 },
 {
  "id": "S056",
  "article_id": "gnews:9f430c6ce2e8d551fe1f063a",
  "title": "DeepSeek Designing Proprietary AI Inference Chip to Cut Nvidia, Huawei Dependence",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek is developing a custom chip designed for AI inference — generating model responses — rather than training, Reuters reported citing three sources [1]\nThe project has been underway for about a year, with DeepSeek quietly recruiting chip engineers and holding talks with foundries and memory suppliers [2]\nNvidia shares fell roughly 1.2% on the day to $193.17, though the stock remains up 22% year-over-year [3]\nDeepSeek recently closed a record $7.5 billion funding round at a valuation above ",
  "passage": "… compress costs [2] .\nThe strategy aligns with DeepSeek's broader cost-efficiency playbook. In May 2026, the company cut prices on its V4-Pro API by 75%, intensifying a price war among Chinese AI providers [5] . Controlling its own silicon would give DeepSeek further leverage to undercut rivals on inference pricing.\nDesigning a competitive AI chip typically takes years and billions of dollars in investment. For a Chinese company, the challenge is compounded by U.S. export controls that bar access to the most advanced overseas foundries and restrict shipments of high-bandwidth memory — a component critical to inference chip performance [2] .\nAnalyst Richard Windey highlighted the constraint: the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China [3] .\nChinese companies are nevertheless accelerating domestic chip adoption. Industry surveys indicate Chinese firms plan to shift 46% of their AI-accelerator budgets to domestic suppliers within 12 months, up from roughly 30% today [5] . DeepSeek's chip effort fits squarely within that trend.\nDeepSeek is not the only AI lab pursuing proprietary silicon. OpenAI and Anthropic have both disclosed custom chip programs, while Google has long run its TPU chips for both training and inference. Amazon designs its Trainium and Inferentia chips for AWS customers [2] .\nWithin China, Huawei's Ascend line of AI accelerators has emerged as the primary domestic alternative to Nvidia. DeepSeek's in-house…",
  "statement": "the same export rules that have effectively zeroed out Nvidia's China revenue also limit DeepSeek's ability to manufacture or sell advanced silicon outside China"
 },
 {
  "id": "S057",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3c81e4fb573a723e4081dd0",
  "title": "One of America's oldest manufacturers says AI is creating jobs — not replacing them",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "By Amanda Macias Fox News\nInside Corning's North Carolina factory, workers are producing the optical fiber that has become a critical piece of the infrastructure powering the AI boom. (Footage courtesy of Corning)\nBefore Henry Ford rolled out the Model T, before the Wright brothers took to the skies and before the Statue of Liberty welcomed millions to America's shores, Corning was already charting a course of innovation that continues today.\nBest known to many Americans for the glass products t",
  "passage": "…tical manufacturing capacity tenfold.\nHaving worked with innovators such as Apple co-founder Steve Jobs during his career, Weeks said he's proud to now partner with NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang as Corning helps build the infrastructure powering the AI revolution.\nWhile companies like NVIDIA have become synonymous with artificial intelligence, Weeks said many people overlook the role optical fiber plays in connecting AI systems.\n\"The common story is AI being powered by chips, but actually, those chips are connected by glass,\" Weeks said.\nThe AI manufacturing boom isn't just fueling growth at longtime American companies like Corning. It's also attracting foreign investment into U.S. manufacturing. Wistron, a Taiwan-based electronics manufacturer, is establishing AI supercomputer manufacturing operations in Texas for NVIDIA, bringing advanced manufacturing jobs to the state.\n\"Building in America is essential for speed, resilience and strategic advantage,\" Wistron Chairman Simon Lin told Fox News Digital.\n\"Texas offers the talent, industrial strength, and strategic location to help power the next generation of AI infrastructure, while creating durable, high-value jobs at scale for the local workforce,\" he added.\nAs America marks 250 years of independence, Weeks said the country's next chapter of innovation will depend not only on breakthrough ideas, but on manufacturing them at home.\n\"The tools change, but the approach doesn't,\" Weeks said.\nAmanda Macias covers the intersection of business, economics and politi…",
  "statement": "Wistron, a Taiwan-based electronics manufacturer, is establishing AI supercomputer manufacturing operations in Texas for NVIDIA"
 },
 {
  "id": "S058",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…ayers with signal integrity, thermal tolerance, and yield at volume is a hard engineering problem, and SemiAnalysis frames it as the gating item for NVIDIA’s most ambitious system.\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits. Data Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27, and CEO Jensen Huang described “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history”. The bottleneck is assembly.\nAMD ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has the clearest window. Shares are up 157.77% year to date, and Goldman Sachs raised its AMD price target to $640 from $450 on July 6. CEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs. Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion. Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion. A Kyber slip strengthens the in-house silicon case.\nNVIDIA has not confirmed the delay. The affected product is a 2028 item; Blackwell and current Rubin systems remain in short supply. NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysi…",
  "statement": "customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations"
 },
 {
  "id": "S059",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\nAI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to…",
  "statement": "Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing"
 },
 {
  "id": "S060",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…damage it is causing in Taiwan, a critical manufacturing base for Nvidia that hosts more than 90 percent of global leading-edge chip manufacturing.\nRiding the wave of the global AI frenzy led by generative AI breakthroughs, Nvidia’s AI GPU microchips have made it the world’s most valuable company, and Huang one of the wealthiest people on the planet. For every $100 spent by U.S. AI companies, $60 is spent on Nvidia’s GPUs, Huang estimated .\nIn the United States and other countries, anger is rising at the prodigious energy and water demands of gargantuan AI data centers. But there is a hidden cost: the toxic supply chain that manufactures advanced semiconductors that fill those data centers. Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled , and the trend is likely to continue as the company’s GPU shipments are forecast to grow at 26 percent per year.\nNvidia outsources most of its manufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of t…",
  "statement": "Over the past three years, Nvidia’s supply chain emissions have more than doubled"
 },
 {
  "id": "S061",
  "article_id": "gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9",
  "title": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe｜Daily News Digest｜2026-07-02｜web only",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe\nJuly 2, 2026 -- Today’s top stories: Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe, Rubio Would Step Aside for Vance in 2028 White House Race, and Google Ordered To Pay Klarna $1.97b in Antitrust Damages.\nBy CommonWealth Magazine\nSingapore seizes $42m home in Nvidia chip smuggling case\nSingapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected c",
  "passage": "…leged that Google was giving preferential treatment to its own price-comparison service in its search results. The case relates to Google’s actions in Pricerunner markets in the UK, Sweden, and Denmark between 2008 and 2023.\nThe antitrust damages mark a partial win for Klarna, which had sought “ significantly higher ” compensation of close to $8Bn, the court said on Wednesday.\n“ Pricerunner is considered to have suffered damage as a result of Google having illegally favoured its price comparison service for many years, ” the Stockholm Patent and Market Court said in a statement. The case relates to Google’s actions in Pricerunner markets in the UK, Sweden, and Denmark between 2008 and 2023.\nSouth Korea to Invest Nearly $1.2 Trillion in Chips and AI Data Centers",
  "statement": "South Korea to Invest Nearly $1.2 Trillion in Chips and AI Data Centers"
 },
 {
  "id": "S062",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout",
  "title": "NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑s",
  "passage": "…rs, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.",
  "statement": "AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally"
 },
 {
  "id": "S063",
  "article_id": "gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d",
  "title": "Nvidia offers revenue sharing model for aspiring AI start-ups",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia offers revenue sharing model for aspiring AI start-ups\nThe aim of its new revenue-sharing model is to build business among researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources.\n(July 2): Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales.\nThe company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, sim",
  "passage": "Nvidia offers revenue sharing model for aspiring AI start-ups\nThe aim of its new revenue-sharing model is to build business among researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources.\n(July 2): Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales.\nThe company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \"usagelinked earnings stream\" for itself, chief financial officer Colette Kress wrote in a blog post on Wednesday. The aim of its new revenue-sharing model is to build business among researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources.\nUnder the company's branding of DSX AI factories, an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider like Firmus Technologies with greater speed and flexibility than would otherwise be the case, Kress said.\n\"For mod…",
  "statement": "granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales"
 },
 {
  "id": "S064",
  "article_id": "gnews:b48593184704bbbe6eddbc03",
  "title": "As chip sector takes it on the chin, traders bet on a big Nvidia rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "Is tech's biggest sleeping giant about to wake up?\nShares of Nvidia fought into the green despite a 5% sell-off in chip stocks tracked by the VanEck Semiconductor ETF (SMH) , a notable outperformance a day after a research report claiming the AI leader was at least a year behind on manufacturing of its next line of server racks.\nThe stock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned t",
  "passage": "…ock's trading just under $200, a level it's flirted with since late last month, down 17% from its May record and up just 4% on the year, as investors have turned their attention to other AI components, like the memory-makers.\nOne thing Nvidia has those stocks don't right now: bullish options flows.\nMore than 1.5 million calls traded in Nvidia Tuesday, compared to under 690,000 puts, with more than twice as many calls bought versus puts bought, according to ThinkorSwim data. Volume ratios are about the opposite in the sector ETF, with puts outpacing calls almost four-to-one in SMH, with traders buying 33,000 puts, compared to just 7,300 calls.\nSimilar action took place in Nvidia Monday after the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays. Calls more than doubled puts by volume with about two-thirds of the $600M of NVDA options premium tied to calls, and almost three times as many calls bought versus puts.\nOne group of trades that looked like they were initiated by a single trader bought a total $3.5 million of the 200-strike calls expiring at the end of July. Those contracts cost just under 7 bucks each at the time of the trade, meaning they still need about 5.5% more to pay off by month-end.\nIt looks like traders are hoping Nvidia's two-day firming will turn into a rally: as of writing the top five contracts are all calls expiring Wednesday. The most popular was the 200-strike, which traded almost 170,000 times for a total…",
  "statement": "the company disputed a report by SemiAnalysis that its next-gen Kyber server rack was running into delays"
 },
 {
  "id": "S065",
  "article_id": "gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6",
  "title": "Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accele",
  "passage": "… years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\nBeyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032. Firmus will still need to identify a datacenter provider or build their own datacenters before they can deploy GPUs.\nWe have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\nNvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\nThere are many more deals in the works and we believe that many of the clusters announced to be built in partnership with Nvidia will later be confirmed to be built atop an Nvidia backstop. In the next section, we will explore how this revenue backstop will affect Nvidia’s balance sheet and income statement.",
  "statement": "AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity"
 },
 {
  "id": "S066",
  "article_id": "gnews:c1658c3cc3b216fa2b0eaa54",
  "title": "Valve is bringing SteamOS to Nvidia GPUs, proving the Steam Machine was never the real plan",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "By Tanveer Singh\nAfter a 7-year corporate stint, Tanveer found his love for writing and tech too much to resist. An MBA in Marketing and the owner of a PC building business, he writes on PC hardware, technology, and Windows. When not scouring the web for ideas, he can be found building PCs, watching anime, or playing Smash Karts on his RTX 3080 (sigh).\nThe Steam Machine was helpless against the dark times we're experiencing in the PC hardware market. Launching at a price that won't do any favors",
  "passage": "…on AMD hardware. This is why Nvidia users face an uphill task when trying to get SteamOS to run as well on their machines, often foregoing SteamOS for alternative distros like Bazzite for better support.\nNow, however, Valve has confirmed that it has a growing team closely working with Team Green to bring official support for Nvidia GPUs. This comes from Valve engineer Pierre-Loup Griffais himself, who said in his interview with Verge that the company is working with AMD, Intel, and Nvidia to make the SteamOS experience better on a wider range of hardware. In his Bluesky post , he also said, \"If you have an AMD GPU, you can build your own Steam Machine now! More GPU support being worked on.\" With the SteamOS installation image updated to version 3.8.10 , Valve has expanded support to more AMD-powered handhelds and AMD discrete GPUs. The Nvidia rollout, however, will take time, as noted by Griffais. He doesn't expect anything before 2027, so Nvidia users have to wait, relying on Bazzite in the meantime.\nReleasing SteamOS from the AMD bubble is a big deal\nNvidia GPUs rule the discrete graphics market\nIt's not a surprise that SteamOS is closely tied to AMD hardware. The Steam Deck was responsible for SteamOS' modern avatar , and the majority of handhelds on the market still run on AMD silicon. Other Linux distros like Bazzite and CachyOS offer better support for Nvidia GPUs, but over 90% of PC gamers using Team Green hardware still feel like second-class citizens on SteamOS. This might finally change as Valve recognizes t…",
  "statement": "With the SteamOS installation image updated to version 3.8.10 , Valve has expanded support to more AMD-powered handhelds and AMD discrete GPUs."
 },
 {
  "id": "S067",
  "article_id": "gnews:4f3a1d09e90b8fec9c86d2ba",
  "title": "Nvidia RTX 5090 GPUs are so expensive that Intel's Arc Pro B70 is now a genuine bargain for AI — 128GB 4-card configuration costs less than $3800",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia RTX 5090 GPUs are so expensive that Intel's Arc Pro B70 is now a genuine bargain for AI — 128GB 4-card configuration costs less than $3800\nBy Rahim Amir\nA memory-centric bargain?\nNvidia 's RTX 5090 is a uniquely capable GPU - the company's highest-end consumer-grade GPU to date features a whopping 21,760 CUDA cores and 32GB of GDDR7 memory in its cut-down GB202 configuration.\nIt also happens to be one of the most expensive GPUs retail consumers can buy in 2026, going for as much as two ti",
  "passage": "…irements), a DRAM crisis is making it impossible for many to secure a capable GPU for their local AI needs, even as one option seems largely ignored: Intel 's Arc Pro B70.\nMost consumers do not consider Intel a GPU maker, associating it instead with consumer- and enterprise-grade CPUs. This is despite the chipmaker offering some of the best value-for-money option s in the mid-range segment it currently competes in.\nIntel's Arc Pro offerings, however, are unapologetically different; They focus exclusively on professional-grade, or rather, AI-centric workloads while skipping any pretense of catering to gaming consumers, even as they remain capable of running most titles one can throw at them.\nThe Intel Arc Pro B70 is its highest-end offering and commands a reference price of $950, with most retailers and OEM partners selling SKUs at around $1,000. With 32GB of GDDR6 memory in tow and a price that is effectively a quarter of most RTX 5090 SKUs on sale at the time of writing, it holds its own as a value-centric alternative to Nvidia's Blackwell-based behemoth.\nIt leverages Intel's BMG-G31 \"Big Battlemage\" chip that was originally slated to show on its since-canceled Arc B770 GPU , as it aims to address a vacuum in the local AI space that both AMD and Nvidia are not willing to address given their focus on maximizing profits at the highest end of the spectrum with enterprise consumers.\nFor those interested in looking at setting up 4 of the Arc Pro B70, Puget Systems has…",
